module gunbc.plans.deploy_convergence_observed_side


fn deploy_convergence_observed_side_body() -> List<std.markdown.MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    p(text: "**Status:** design record, 2026-08-20. Reached by two independent derivations plus an adversarial exchange across two sessions. Most of its value is in the DEAD ENDS: the observed-provider design below was fully specified, then refuted, and the next person to look at live_deploy's empty observed side will re-derive it unless this record stops them."),

    h2(text: "The defect that starts it"),
    p(text: "gunbc.live_deploy.emit calls the grain-agnostic membership_reconcile in both directions, but only at its DEGENERATE POLES -- apply passes an empty observed list, retract passes an empty desired list. So live_deploy has no observed provider and cannot converge; it can only install-everything or remove-everything. That is why a redeploy has always been the only remedy for any drift, and why a missing toolchain precondition went unnoticed for the workflow's entire life: an apply-all pole never asks what is already true, so it has nowhere to notice a precondition either."),

    h2(text: "REFUTED: supplying an observed provider does not close the drift loop"),
    p(text: "The obvious repair -- observe the host, feed the apply pole, stop being degenerate -- was designed in full and is WRONG. The member value is a function of its own key, so the diff cannot see staleness."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**The vacuity:** DeploymentArtifactStep carries only kind and path. key_of returns the path. value_eq is whole-value structural equality. So for any matching KEY the only remaining field is kind, which the spec fixes per path -- value_eq cannot distinguish anything and MemberChanged is UNREACHABLE. The diff is a set-membership check, not a state comparison."),
      li(text: "**The consequence, and it is a regression:** every artifact path on srv1 already exists; only their CONTENT is stale. A non-degenerate apply therefore produces an EMPTY PLAN and deploys nothing, reporting converged over a stale host -- where today's add-all unconditionally rsyncs and actually deploys. The repair would have been the silently-converged failure, reached through the fix rather than through the thing being fixed."),
      li(text: "**Why the member cannot be taught:** DeploymentSpec carries WHERE (topology, stable addresses); CandidateRelease carries WHAT (revision, expected surface identity). That separation is correct. Making members candidate-sensitive is exactly what would make MemberChanged reachable -- which activates EffectReplace, which the emitter deliberately models as impossible and answers with a loud poison rather than guessing. Replacement behaviour would have to be defined for every member kind first. That is a substantial modelling project, not a step."),
      li(text: "**Receipt for the blindness:** tree_sync_was_a_host_singleton_note already records that the sync transport files are not paths in DeploymentArtifactStep, so a claim over declared members could not see them."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "SUPERSEDED: bind tree-sync to revision standing"),
    p(text: "**This section proposed the shallow closer and it is WRONG. It is rewritten here rather than deleted, because it was the reasoning that survived one refutation and then failed a second, and a record that shows only the surviving answer teaches nothing about how the wrong one looked defensible.** What it said: the drift question is answered by revisions, the authority already exists and already refuses correctly, so the only missing piece is that nothing ACTS on the drifted arm -- needing no member work, no observed provider, no new decision model, riding srv1's existing enrolled converge step."),
    p(text: "Two later findings each independently kill it. **First, revision standing is one axis and not deployed-tree convergence:** the tree-sync leg copies the working tree and selected .git state separately, so a target whose HEAD equals the desired revision while its tracked bytes do not is representable, and RevisionConverged reports it as converged. **Second, and worse, a tree-only catch-up is not merely incomplete but actively unsafe** -- it moves local HEAD to the desired revision and thereby flips the belt's admission gate from refusing to admitting while the installed binary is still the old one, which the section below records in full."),
    p(text: "What survives from it is exactly one observation, and it is still true: RevisionDrifted carries BOTH desired and local, and the converge_cli carrier note says that is precisely why -- a catch-up needs the prior. That local value is a compare-and-swap BASELINE. It is not the deployment truth, and treating it as the whole answer was the error."),


    h2(text: "STANDING: retract must never take a host observation"),
    p(text: "Ownership is a claim about HISTORY -- who wrote this. An observation returns PRESENT STATE. Two files of identical shape and different origin are indistinguishable to any present-state read, so a host-observed retract asks the observation for a fact it structurally does not contain, and every answer is a plausible fabrication with delete on the other end. The poles need ownership asymmetrically: apply never needs provenance because desired bounds what it writes; retract needs it for every member because Removed is irreversible. So the degenerate retract pole is not a limitation awaiting a fix -- it is correct, and spec-driven is its right permanent source. Release identity does not rescue it: that is provenance for the running PROCESS, not for bytes on disk, and an install manifest is ownership STORED, which the ownership authority deliberately refused."),

    h2(text: "SHELVED, and NOT blocked by the refutation above"),
    p(text: "The census answers a question nothing in the tree answers today -- what unowned state is on srv1 -- and it never touches value_eq, MemberChanged, EffectReplace, or the member model. It was split from the diff observation precisely so neither could contaminate the other, and only the diff half died."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**Two observations, never one.** A diff observation shaped as a per-desired-member query cannot construct an observed-only key, so Removed stays unreachable on apply STRUCTURALLY rather than by a filter the provider remembers to apply. A census observation enumerates host state, feeds nothing into the diff, and is the only thing that can see beyond the roster. The property that makes each safe is the other's disqualification."),
      li(text: "**Class grain, not instance grain.** Worktree registrations are created per dispatch, so an instance-keyed contract refuses every time an agent session starts -- a gate that fires on normal operation gets silenced within a day, manufacturing the escape hatch out of a contract."),
      li(text: "**n equals one is PROVISIONAL.** One observation cannot distinguish an instance from a singleton class. A single observed path licenses no refusal and is not baselined; it becomes a class when a second instance appears or when a typed disposition names it. Three transitions are recorded: provisional to class, class to wider class, class to absent."),
      li(text: "**The control gates the baseline, not merely accompanies it.** The baseline is a measurement of the current host, which DESIGN 5 names as not-an-oracle; the monotone-debt carve-out admits it only if the universe is independently discovered. So the census must first be shown to have seen the worktree-registration grain, and if it has not, NOTHING may be baselined."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "THE TREE-PUBLICATION CLOSER: what exists, what is missing, and the two traps"),
    p(text: "The deploy threads the admitted revision to the artifact that ANNOUNCES which release it is and not to the artifact that DETERMINES it. gunbc.live_deploy.emit emit_artifact_upsert takes a ReleaseRevisionBinding; its ServeBinary arm consumes it, and its GunbcSourceTree arm sources the tree from the ambient working directory and never reads it. Readiness cannot catch this: the process-reported revision and the expected revision both derive from the same admitted value, so they agree by construction whatever the tree contains -- the evidence layer verifies the announcement, and the announcement is not derived from the thing announced."),
    p(text: "The consequence is a NON-CONVERGING LOOP rather than a false converged, and the distinction is operational. gunbc.fleet_desired_observe fleet_revision_standing reads the deployed tree back, and the .git leg whitelists HEAD, so a tree published from a wrong-revision checkout reports RevisionDrifted correctly and forever -- republishing an arbitrary tree each cycle while the gate visibly never converges, which is the state that invites someone to disable the gate."),
    p(text: "**This is a ceiling finding, not a discovery.** gunbc.live_deploy.deployed_tree_scope states in its own header that the deployed source is the working tree and not the commit, and the candidate-admission machinery downstream exists precisely because of it. What that reasoning never considers is the checkout being at the WRONG COMMIT: it classifies paths, and every path in a wrong-revision checkout is an ordinary tracked path. The mitigation is complete for the class it was designed against and structurally blind to this one."),

    h2(text: "The closer's dependency list, after both halves were measured"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**EXISTS, do not rebuild.** deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes is already the single row, already deriving BOTH deployed_tree_rsync_exclude_flags and path_is_deployed, already witnessed segment-wise. path_is_deployed IS the projection membership function an identity computation needs."),
      li(text: "**IDENTITY IS OVER THE PROJECTION, never the tree.** The deployed bytes are an rsync projection, so a digest of the source tree and a digest of the deployed directory are unequal BY CONSTRUCTION and every run reports drift forever -- which fails safe but is indistinguishable from real drift, so it is silenced. The exclude set therefore stops being a copy filter and becomes part of the definition of sameness, and a change to it is a typed mass re-baseline. The identity function must DERIVE the set from the same authority the rsync argv derives from, never take it as a caller-supplied parameter, or the caller-property class is rebuilt inside the fix meant to dissolve it."),
      li(text: "**The .git leg stays OUT of the byte-identity subject.** It is answering a different question: it exists so the deployed tree is self-describing. Its claim is that HEAD names the admitted revision and that revision is REACHABLE in the deployed .git -- one-directional, decidable, and immune to the additivity that makes equality impossible. Reachability is the claim one would want even with --delete, so this is not a concession."),
      li(text: "**MISSING: an on-disk tree digest.** The existing readback (live_deploy.apply) is keyed on the listen PORT and reads the served surface over HTTP -- the right instrument for readiness and the wrong subject for tree identity, because a served-surface digest cannot distinguish a correct tree from a process rendering a tree that is no longer there."),
      li(text: "**MISSING: a remote revision observation.** git.Core.RevParseInRepo declares transport shell with argv git -C REPO rev-parse TARGET and takes no host, so fleet_revision_standing can answer about exactly one host: the one the process runs on. That is a ceiling on the observation, not a caller property that happens to hold."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "Why the remote observation is an ARGV lift and not a second operation"),
    p(text: "Multi-transport binding is real and in use in extdeps, so the §3 prescription -- bind another handler to the existing shape rather than fork the operation -- is the right instinct. But there is no ssh transport KIND anywhere in the corpus (240 shell, 72 rest, 9 handler, 6 file). Remoteness here is an OPERATION, ssh.Session.ExecPortableWords, reached through a FleetSshExecutionContext carrying the credential, the attempt-owned known-hosts anchor and the portable-word allowlist. That is a context threaded, not a handler bound, and teaching the transport layer to carry host-key verification would rebuild exactly the failure ExecPortableWords was built to correct."),
    p(text: "So the forked thing is one level below the operation: it is the ARGV, hardwired inside a transport literal and therefore unreachable to anything that is not that transport. Lift the words to their own authority -- the same move deployed_tree_excluded_prefixes already made -- and the shell transport renders them locally while shape_fleet_ssh_exec carries the same words remotely. One authority, two consumers, no RevParseInRepoRemote nickname, no new transport kind, no caller choosing between two spellings of one concept. Minting the -Remote variant is the forbidden move and the obvious one."),

    h2(text: "THE PAIR MUST SHARE ONE EXECUTION CONTEXT, decided before either is built"),
    p(text: "The two remote observations -- tree digest and revision -- are built separately and joined into one verdict. If each takes its own host parameter, the join can read the revision off host A and the digest off host B and report a convergence about neither, and it would be perfectly well typed: two host-parameterized observations, one join, no refusal available. That is the same fusion this record is about, relocated from inside an operation to the plan layer."),
    p(text: "The construction form is that neither observation takes a host. Both are taken THROUGH one FleetSshExecutionContext, and the join consumes observations carrying that context's identity, so a cross-host join has no constructor rather than being refused by a check. This is cheap now, while both are being written and can share the carrier, and expensive later, because retrofitting means changing both signatures and every call site. The caller sentence for the version one would otherwise write -- this verdict is about one host provided both observations were given the same host argument -- is non-empty and unenforced, which is the whole test."),
    p(text: "This row is the first instance of the class recorded as a PREDICTION rather than as a post-mortem: the check ran ahead of the defect instead of behind it. That is the transferable result of the thread, more than any single site it found."),

    h2(text: "BLOCKING: a tree-only catch-up ENABLES the belt, and a nonzero exit has no authority over a timer"),
    p(text: "Verified on three legs. gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate belt_revision_admission matches RevisionConverged, RevisionDrifted and RevisionUnobserved and reads nothing else -- no executable identity enters the decision -- and it is fed fleet_revision_standing over the instance repo root. GunbcSourceTree and ServeBinary are SEPARATE arms of emit_artifact_upsert, so the tree and the installed binary move independently. The belt then runs the installed binary with its working directory set to that same repo root."),
    p(text: "So a tree-only catch-up performs this transition: before, desired D with tree, binary and process all at L, and the belt REFUSES because L is not D; after, tree D with binary and process still at L, and the belt ADMITS because D equals D. Readiness correctly reports the mismatch and the run exits nonzero, and it does not matter -- the belt is an independent timer, and on its next tick it dispatches binary L executing against source D. A mixed realization never admitted as a release, running in production, admitted by a gate whose premise the catch-up just falsified."),
    p(text: "**This is why the spark refusal precedent does not transfer.** Refuse, exit nonzero, put the receipt in the failure reason is a correct VERDICT pattern, and it is not fail-closed when the mutation performed BEFORE the refusal enables a separate actuator. A failed spark transition leaves a bad state nothing else consumes; this one changes the input to another actuator's gate, and an exit status has no authority over a systemd timer."),
    p(text: "And it is this record's own class once more: repository revision equality is a sufficient dispatch admission was true only while the executable and the tree always moved together, which was true only because apply-all moved everything at once. Tree-only catch-up is precisely the change that falsifies it. The belt gate asserts as a property of revision equality what is really a property of how deployment currently behaves."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "**SUPERSEDED, and it was insufficient in a way that would have shipped: quiescing the belt TIMER leaves the other ingress live.** `belt_revision_admission` is consumed by TWO actuators -- the timer tick and the direct dispatch path -- and the corpus already says so in its own note: the belt timer is a dispatch actuator exactly as much as the button is. Stopping `gunbc-...belt.timer` would have left the click path admitting against a half-published release, which is the same defect this row exists to prevent, reintroduced by the fix for it. The seam is the shared admission fold, not either ingress. Corrected below."),
      li(text: "**The real fix: executable identity enters belt admission.** Larger, durable, and filed rather than done here."),
      li(text: "**A lock spanning standing re-read through read-back.** The deployment env file is per-deployment and shared and fleet-converge declares no concurrency group, so two catch-up runs interleave: A writes source A, B overwrites source B, A restarts the unit and it reads B. Pre-check and post-check cannot close that."),
      li(text: "**Readiness must run on the NO-OP path.** If a run skips readiness because no tree action occurred, an earlier failed run manufactures a future false green: plan empty, standing converged, old process still serving, nothing looks."),
    ]),

    h2(text: "RULING: where quiescence belongs, and why neither obvious placement is complete"),
    p(text: "Three placements, and the question was which one -- the answer is that they are three different boundaries and all three are required. **At the OPERATION boundary**, a publication plan must be unconstructible without a guard proving release-consuming actuators are quiesced: the guard is a required field, so an unguarded publication has no constructor rather than being refused by a check. **At the PARENT TRANSACTION boundary**, that same frozen plan must acquire the quiescence, publish, verify, and restore actuation automatically -- because a guard supplied from outside would leave the fleet permanently undeployable (the belt is normally enabled), so someone disables it, retries, and re-enables by hand, which is out-of-band convergence wearing a safety label. **In the STEADY-STATE model**, belt admission must consume a coherent release identity rather than a repository revision."),
    p(text: "**The objection that made this look like a new ownership claim was wrong, and it was mine.** `BeltTimerUnit` is already a live-deploy OWNED artifact kind -- its upsert installs and enables the unit, its teardown disables and removes it -- so deployment already owns that lifecycle. What changes is not who owns it but that the dependency becomes honest instead of relying on apply-all moving everything fast enough."),
    p(text: "**The fourth requirement sits underneath all three: the quiescence authority must span admission THROUGH actuation.** A check performed before a dispatch, followed by an unrelated mutation, is another time-of-check/time-of-use gap -- a tick that passed admission a moment before deploy acquired quiescence still executes during publication. So two mechanisms are needed, not one: a PERSISTENT inhibition that survives process failure, so a deploy that dies after publishing the tree and before restarting the service leaves the belt still refusing and the next converge resumes or rolls back with no operator re-enabling anything; and an EXECUTION LEASE that both sides hold, deploy exclusively from its final pre-mutation observation through read-back, every release-consuming belt path shared from its standing read through operation commitment. A lock that serializes only two deployment runs does not protect against the third actuator."),
    p(text: "**Two live-tree facts pin the shape.** `belt_revision_admission` is reached from exactly two call sites -- the tick and the dispatch path -- which is why it is the right single seam to replace rather than either ingress being the right thing to stop. And `BeltRunState.Paused` is NOT a live host control: production passes `roadmap_belt_default_run_state`, a constant `Running`, with no observed member or actuator changing it, so repurposing that pure planner input as quiescence would be a name borrowed for a mechanism that does not exist."),
    p(text: "The release subject is a BINDING rather than any single identity: binary B executes tree T under contract C. Executable identity alone is insufficient for the same reason revision alone is -- the failure being prevented is a coherent-looking pair of individually-correct facts about different releases."),

    h2(text: "The instrument is git -- and BOTH of the first two spellings were wrong"),
    p(text: "The portable-word alphabet forbids the pipe, so a remote content digest cannot be a shell pipeline. That constraint pointed at git itself, which is right: git is already the content-identity authority for the tracked set, and the tracked set is exactly what the working-tree rsync leg ships. No digest, no second content domain to normalize, no dependency on the exclusion authority, and the projection trap dissolves rather than being solved."),
    p(text: "**But `git status` cannot work on a tree-synced host, and the reason is one line of the .git whitelist.** That leg syncs HEAD, objects, packed-refs and refs; its own carrier note lists what never crosses, and the list contains INDEX. So after a CORRECT sync the deployed repo is in a state git never produces on its own: HEAD and objects at the new revision, worktree at the new revision, and .git/index still recording whatever the target held before. git status compares HEAD to index to worktree, so with a stale index every changed path reports modified in BOTH columns, permanently, on a host that is exactly right -- a comparison that can never return equal, which is the same vacuity the digest had, arriving inside the replacement meant to dissolve it."),
    p(text: "**Measured, not reasoned.** A scratch repo at D with its index restored from L: `status --porcelain --untracked-files=no` reports `MM f.txt`, while the identical repo with a correct index reports nothing. Four candidates were then driven against that state in both directions -- status, diff-index, diff-files and `git diff --name-only HEAD` -- and only the last one discriminates: empty when the host is right, naming the path when a tracked file is actually wrong, empty again on restore. It compares worktree CONTENT against HEAD's tree and tolerates the stale index. Every word passes the alphabet."),
    p(text: "It also dissolves the untracked question instead of managing it with a flag: `git diff HEAD` has no untracked concept, so the subject is exactly whether the tracked bytes equal this commit, with no option for a later reader to simplify away. Untracked-but-shipped stays candidate admission's class, which already owns and refuses it. **That last sentence was WRONG and is retained as the record of the reasoning that produced the defect** -- candidate admission owns the source checkout and nothing owned the target; see the scope-defect section below, which is the repair."),
    p(text: "**The silent direction was tested and does not reach this deploy.** git may skip re-hashing when stat data matches the index, and rsync -t preserves mtimes, which would be a false CLEAN -- the direction that fails toward good news and goes unnoticed. But git's stat cache records ctime as well, and ctime cannot be set by touch or preserved by rsync: a synced file's ctime is the sync time, so the cache never matches and git re-hashes unconditionally. A deliberate same-size, mtime-restored content change was caught by both commands. The property that makes the index stale is the same one that blocks the silent failure."),
    p(text: "**One precondition remains, clean today and with an enforceable home.** `git diff HEAD` compares through gitattributes, so a clean/smudge filter, text=auto, eol= or working-tree-encoding would report clean while the deployed bytes differ by normalization -- and rsync ships bytes. Measured: zero occurrences of those attributes; .gitattributes carries only linguist-language (presentation) and merge=generated-artifact (merge driver). Unlike the other preconditions this one has an AUTHORITY rather than only an inspection -- the file is generated from gunbc.gitattributes_emit -- so a refusal to emit a normalizing attribute makes the premise unwritable instead of true-by-inspection. Filed beside the instrument, landing with the vertical."),
    p(text: "**Three instrument candidates were killed by the alphabet, and that is the constraint doing useful work rather than forcing a compromise:** each one it rejected was ALSO semantically wrong. The digest needed a pipeline and compared two projections; status needed a flag and consults an artifact the sync deliberately omits; `git diff HEAD` needs neither and asks the actual question. What the alphabet does NOT address, and what is worth doing separately, is the refusal: a nonzero exit is currently a String cause, and not-a-git-repository and permission-denied-on-.git deserve different arms."),

    h2(text: "THE SCOPE DEFECT: the observation claimed the tracked set and the sync ships more than that"),
    p(text: "**An earlier revision of the observation read `--untracked-files=no` and its own note dispositioned untracked-but-shipped to candidate admission as a different class. That disposition was false, and it was false toward good news.** Candidate admission owns the SOURCE checkout. Nothing owned the TARGET. The sync leg publishes the working directory wholesale modulo the deployment exclusion authority, so a host could carry HEAD equal to the desired commit, every tracked file clean, and an untracked or ignored `.dag` source sitting inside the deployed scope -- and the observation would have reported converged while the bytes the compiler actually reads were not the ones that commit names. Candidate admission already treats exactly this class as load-bearing on its own side and refuses on it, which is what makes the target-side silence a gap rather than a difference of opinion."),
    p(text: "The repair reads the same two argv surfaces the source side reads -- `status --porcelain=v1 -z --untracked-files=all` and `ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard -z` -- and scans them with `scan_checkout_status` and `scan_ignored_deployed_paths` THEMSELVES rather than a second scanner for one grammar. Membership projects through `path_is_deployed`, so the two sides cannot disagree about what is in scope. Both legs run under the same temporary index, because `ls-files --others` consults the index to decide what other means and the deployed index is the artifact this module refuses to trust."),
    p(text: "**The refusal came with it.** A scan that cannot parse its input now refuses instead of answering clean: a malformed porcelain field and a list ending owing a rename source are both `ContentUnreadable`, carried into a `TreeStatusUnreadable` cause. Rendering either as an empty change set would be the empty-observation narrow -- the deploy proceeds because nothing was found, when nothing was readable."),
    p(text: "**The discriminating control is the one that makes the two new witnesses mean something.** An untracked `target/release/gunbc` and an ignored `target/debug/x` must stay CONVERGED, because they are outside the deployed scope by the same authority and they are present on every real host. Without it, both new witnesses are satisfied by a scanner that calls every non-empty reading diverged. Twenty-eight witnesses green on the observation module, eight on the release binding, six on the report."),
    p(text: "**What the repair did NOT close, named rather than left to be rediscovered:** the four legs are one per-HOST observation and not one per-TIME observation. The sealed locus makes a cross-host join unrepresentable; it does not make the readings atomic. The temp index narrows the race in the safe direction -- the status and ignored legs are bound to the revision the FIRST leg observed, so a mid-flight sync presents as divergence rather than as false clean -- but a worktree that moves fully to another revision reads as MODIFIED at a revision it no longer occupies. Fail-safe in direction, still wrong in fact. Rung: mitigatable. Next-rung trigger: the deployment execution lease, which publication requires anyway, so the atomic read arrives as a property of the lease rather than as a second mechanism. Building a porcelain v2 scanner first would mint a second grammar for one concept that the lease then obsoletes."),

    h2(text: "THE NEXT CUT, SIZED HONESTLY: what the observable pair already closes, and what would be invented"),
    p(text: "The directed steady-state model is `DeploymentReleaseIdentity` over four axes -- candidate revision, source tree identity, executable identity, service contract identity -- with belt admission consuming a coherent release rather than a revision. **Two of those four axes have no observer in this tree today.** Nothing reads the installed binary's content hash, and nothing reads a service contract identity. Minting a four-field record where two fields can only be filled by an authored value is the unconsumed modeling slice this program is under standing instruction not to add, and it is also how a carrier acquires a field that is true by construction until the input becomes real."),
    p(text: "**The observable pair covers the PRE-RESTART half of the BLOCKING finding, and this row previously claimed it covered the whole thing. It does not, and the refutation is measured.** The process revision is not read from the executable's bytes -- it is a LAUNCH ARGUMENT: the emitted systemd unit's ExecStart carries `--release-revision <sha>`, verifiable in the unit-emission oracle's frozen expectation. So before a restart an old process still runs under the OLD unit, announces L against a tree at D, and `release_binding_holds` correctly refuses -- that is the transition that motivated the row. But after a restart an old EXECUTABLE relaunched under the NEW unit announces D, and the fold answers bound while the binary bytes are still old. The carrier is a transition guard across the publication window, not a release identity, and it is renamed in its own note rather than left reading as one. **The correction stands rather than being smoothed away, because the overstatement is exactly what stops anyone rechecking.** The next rung needs no invented field: `RunningReleaseObservation` already carries a surface_bundle_identity beside the revision, and that identity IS derived from what the process built rather than from an argument, so consuming it here raises the claim to operational release acceptance. Exact executable provenance remains a declared future refinement, dependent on something observing the binary's bytes -- which is still a reason NOT to author `executable_identity` and `service_contract_identity` today."),
    p(text: "**One §3 question is settled and recorded so it is not re-litigated: the belt's local read and the deploy path's remote read are ONE shape with TWO transports, not two authorities.** The belt runs ON the host and reads its own repo; the deploy path reads a remote host over the sealed SSH locus. DESIGN §3 holds that transport is a Realization handler bound to an interface shape and never a fact about the dependency, so forking the standing into a local type and a remote type would be the N x M adapter trap with two rows. `fleet_revision_standing` is already the local sibling of the remote observation this record adds, and the convergence is to bind both to one shape -- which is also why the remote observation deliberately did not mint a `RevParseInRepoRemote` operation beside the local one."),
    p(text: "**Where this does NOT land: this PR.** The Spark acceptance and the deploy-root replacement are two subjects, and #8377 is already 122 commits across 128 files. Replacing `belt_revision_admission` -- a fold reached from both the timer tick and the click path in a load-bearing production module -- on a branch whose subject is Spark serving would make both unreviewable, and the replacement-migration doctrine wants that cut on its own integration branch where the deletion is the census. Recorded as a directed next step with its vehicle named, not started here."),

    h2(text: "RUNG HONESTY: what the witness count proves, and what it does not"),
    p(text: "Forty-two witnesses are green across the three modules, and every one of them exercises a PURE FOLD -- argv construction, the tracked-state decision, the four-arm standing, the release binding, the typed refusal rendering. Several are discriminating: a mutation consulting tracked state before the revision reds the drift-dominance witness and nothing else; the portability wall is driven with a pipe that must be refused; each arm is asserted distinct rather than each checked alone."),
    p(text: "**The effectful path is NOT proven, and the witness count must not be read as proving it.** No execution of `deployed_tree_report_ci_wet` has occurred against a host. The observation legs, the ssh transport binding, the exit-code handling on a real refusal, and the receipt write are all unexercised. The session that authored this has no fleet credential -- the entry needs the injected agent the spark entries get -- so the gap is not an oversight but the boundary of what could be run from here."),
    p(text: "The commands themselves WERE driven, against real repositories in the states that matter: a scratch repo with a stale index, the same repo with a correct index, a corrupted worktree, and a same-size mtime-restored edit. That establishes the instrument is right. It does not establish that this module invokes it correctly, and those are different claims -- the exact distinction between a verdict proven on paper and one proven by a consumer, which is the failure this whole record is about."),
    p(text: "So the honest rung for the OBSERVATION path is mitigatable: typed outcomes and total functions throughout, with the effectful seam unexercised. Its next-rung trigger is one execution against srv1 producing a receipt, which requires a workflow step -- and that step is the same thing blocked on the scaffold verdict. Recorded here rather than left for a reader to infer from a green witness count."),

    h2(text: "The executable form of the whole class"),
    p(text: "Every reconcile binding must have a control that holds the key fixed, varies meaningful state, and expects MemberChanged. If that control cannot be written, the comparison is vacuous and the binding is a set-membership check wearing a reconcile's clothes. The projection trap is the same test approached from the other end: if identity is computed over the raw tree, the CHANGED arm is the only reachable arm, and a comparison that can never return equal is exactly as uninformative as one that can never return different. Both fail the one test."),

    h2(text: "The class this record is really about"),
    p(text: "A value that was true BY CONSTRUCTION under a degenerate input stops being true when the input becomes real, while the type still asserts it. Seven instances surfaced in one afternoon on this one subject: ownership always Present; Owned meaning ours; the belt revision gate agreeing with itself because both sides pointed at the same hand-set ref; Removed unreachable because the provider filters; residue empty because a per-member query has no walk; a class boundary derived from a common prefix collapsing to instance grain at n equals one; and value_eq meaningful. Every one is a predicate trivially satisfied because one side of a comparison was empty, identical, or determined by the other."),
    p(text: "**The check, which is cheap and which caught the last three:** state in ONE SENTENCE what must remain true about your CALLERS for your fix to hold. If that sentence is non-empty and unwritten, it is the next instance. This is not review-your-own-work: each instance was invisible to its author and obvious to the reader, in both directions, because the thing that makes a fix feel finished is precisely the assumption just made about how it will be called. Faces four, five and six each arrived INSIDE the fix for the previous one."),
  ]
}

data deploy_convergence_observed_side_plan: gunbc.plan.Plan = gunbc.plan.Plan {
  slug: "deploy-convergence-observed-side",
  title: "Deploy convergence: the observed side, and why the obvious repair is a regression",
  body: deploy_convergence_observed_side_body(),
  retirement: gunbc.plan.PlanRetiresWhen {
    condition: unbound_dissolution(
      description: "Delete this record when the tree-publication vertical is live and its lessons have moved to the carriers that enforce them: the deployed-tree observation green-by-execution against srv1 with a receipt; publication constructible only through a quiescence guard that proves the belt TIMER and SERVICE inactive with no queued or running invocation; belt admission consuming a release identity that binds binary to tree rather than repository revision alone; and the recurring class -- a predicate true by construction under a degenerate input, still asserted by its type when the input becomes real -- carried as a row in the guarantee census with its own discriminating control. Until every one of those holds, this is the only place the refuted branch, the instrument history and the belt-enablement finding are written down, and deleting it would cost the next author the same three nights.",
    ),
  },
}
